L000310	Different regions represent different leading order non-zero contributions to the localization length, indicated by the power in the white circles. The scaling with $W^{-2}$ is the usual Anderson localization behavior that one also gets with ordinary (non-stealthy) disorder. The vertical line at $k_0 = \chi = 0$, corresponding to uncorrelated disorder, always exhibits this behavior, as expected.
L000311	
L000312	The inverse localization length can be expressed as an asymptotic series in powers of $W^{2}$ as
L000313	\begin{equation}
L000314	\label{eq:seriesxi}
L000315	    \frac{1}{\xi(k,k_0)} \sim \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_{2n}(k,k_0) \,W^{2n}.
L000316	\end{equation}
L000317	Along the line $k_0 = \chi = 0$, each term in the series expansion of Eq.~\eqref{eq:seriesxi} is non-vanishing.
L000318	By increasing the value of $\chi$, one gets different scalings of the localization length depending on the energy. This means that, for those values of $k$ and $k_0$, some of the lower order terms $a_{2n}(k,k_0)$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:seriesxi} are vanishing.
L000319	In approaching the line $k_0=\pi$, i.e. $\chi = 1/2$, which corresponds to the lattice (no disorder), the index $m$ such that $a_{2n} = 0$ for $n<m$ is increasing, and ultimately $m \to \infty$ for $\chi \to 1/2$.
L000320	
L000321	The series in Eq.~\eqref{eq:seriesxi} is an asymptotic expansion in small $W^2$, which is diverging when non-perturbative effects are excluded, as very often occurs in perturbative calculations~\cite{Dyson1952Divergence}. Once such non-perturbative effects are taken into account, the divergence is removed and the result can be written as a finite number of terms up to $n=N$ plus a small remainder, which is of order $o(W^{2N})$. When doing so, the vanishing of the lower order terms due to the stealthiness condition implies a larger localization length, scaling with the first non-zero term in $W^2$. In the next Section, we will show that this is indeed the case by numerically computing the dependence of the localization length $\xi$ on $W$ at different values of $\chi$, which properly accounts for the remainder terms.
L000322	
L000323	In this section, we have discussed the series of transitions in the leading order contribution to the localization length at small disorder $W$.  In the Supplementary Material, we show that analogous transitions occur in the level splitting of adjacent energy levels that become degenerate as $W \to 0$ due to the $k\to -k$ symmetry.
